  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Big temperature swing ahead with record \nchallenging warmth followed by major cooldown this weekend \n\nAbundant sunshine and gusty southwest breeze fueling record\nchallenging warmth this afternoon across central PA. It's an\nOcTOASTer pattern for sure. The unseasonably mild temperatures\ncontinue tonight with min temps +20 to +25 degree above climo\nin the upper 50s and lower 60s. \n\nIn stark contrast to the summerlike conditions this afternoon, a\nmajor cooldown is looming for the end of the week into the\nweekend. The temperature difference between highs today and\nminimum wind chills Saturday morning will be 50 to 60 degrees!\nFor example, the most extreme case would be around Bradford, PA\nwith a drop from 78F this afternoon to a wind chill of 18F\nSaturday morning. \n\nFriday night could be a wind-driven, advective cold freeze \nsituation (no frost due to gusty winds) across parts of the \nAlleghenies, with a more traditional frost/freeze pattern \nsetting up for Saturday and Sunday nights. \n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Windy and wet end to the week with first flakes\n\nLatest HREF/REFS bring some rain showers from northern Ohio \nacross the southwest part of the forecast area on Wednesday.\nSpotty rain chances should be largely confined to the southern \ntier Wednesday night into early Thursday. Total QPF through 00Z\nFriday ranges from a couple of hundreths across the Endless \nMtns/NEPA to 0.25-0.50\" in the Laurel Highlands. \n\nAn amplified upper trough will cross the Great Lakes Fri-Sat \nand spin up a deep sfc low near the Canadian Maritimes. This\nsystem will bring widespread rain/showers Thursday night into\nFriday before transitioning to a lake effect/enhanced precip\npattern Friday night into early Saturday. Total QPF from 00Z\nFriday to 00Z Saturday ranges from around 0.75\" in the NW mtns\ndownwind of Lake Erie to <0.10\" in the southern tier valleys to\nthe east of I-99 and south of the PA Turnpike. \n\nGusty winds 35-45 mph are forecast on the backside of the deep \nlow and will deliver much colder air downwind of the Great Lakes\nthis weekend. The shot of colder air will allow for rain to mix\nor changeover to snow showers Friday night into Saturday \nmorning across the higher terrain along the Allegheny Mtns. Any \nminor accum (<0.5\") would have to occur overnight and be limited\nto non paved surfaces.",
